ws. The R137 example had 3 model_level attrs out of 7 columns (they skipped cyclic_volume, max_pressure_loss, pmax — which were constant! pmax=50 for both, max_pressure_loss=200 both, cyclic_volume 0.9 both — actually those constant ones went... hmm, in the example they only showed qmax, qt, qmin in model_level; the constant attributes weren't shown but presumably would be type_level). \n\nInteresting: in the R137 example, model_level only includes varying attributes; constant attributes (cyclic_volume, max_pressure_loss, pmax) presumably go to type_level. So following that pattern: accuracy_class varies → model_level. All of q1,q2,q3,q4,ratio vary → model_level. So all 6.\n\nOK let me just do it. To keep the JSON manageable I'll write value objects as {\"model\": \"...\", \"value\": X}.\n\nFor model ids, use \"DN25 class 2 Q3=10\" style. Let me double check uniqueness again: models 16 & 17: DN100 class 1 Q3=160 and Q3=250 — unique. models 12/13 unique. Good.\n\nActually for models 32-36, class \"1 or 2\": model_id \"DN350 class 1 or 2 Q3=2500\".\n\nNow write everything.\n\nOne more thing: `type_designations` — just WATERFLUX 3070. Meter sizes DN25–DN600 aren't type designations per se; they're model variants.\n\nCategory: schema examples list \"Load cell\", \"Indicator\", \"Measuring system\", \"Fuel dispenser\", \"Taximeter\", \"Gas meter\", ... — I'll use \"Water meter\".\n\nLet me also add matrix table for Table 3:\n```\n{\n  \"name\": \"General characteristics of the indicating device\",\n  \"columns\": [\"meter_sizes\", \"indicating_range_minimum_value\", \"verification_scale_interval_maximum\"],\n  \"column_units\": [null, \"m³\", \"m³\"],\n  \"rows\": [\n    {\"meter_sizes\": \"DN25, DN40 and DN50\", \"indicating_range_minimum_value\": 99999999, \"verification_scale_interval_maximum\": 0.0001},\n    {\"meter_sizes\": \"DN65, DN80, DN100, DN125 and DN150\", \"indicating_range_minimum_value\": 99999999, \"verification_scale_interval_maximum\": 0.001},\n    {\"